From: Luma Developers
Subject: [PATCH] Keycap: cached key grid, pressed cap drawn over it, nothing new on a first press

On the Fold a press frame still cost about 18 ms of redraw with a median of
22 Cogl journal flushes, and the first press of a word stalled one frame for
185 ms. Measured headless with Mutter's own profiler, nearly every press
frame ran a dozen journal flushes, all from the key preview: the preview of
a character not shown before was laid out, rasterised and rendered into
St.Label's offscreen text cache in the press frame, because the step meant
to draw everything ahead of time after the keyboard opens ran from the show
animation's onComplete, which a relayout stops, so it never ran.

- Each key layer is cached as one texture (offscreen redirect) and a press
  never invalidates it: the pressed cap is drawn over the grid by one small
  group (a tray-coloured cover, a clone of the cap lowered by the Keycap
  travel with its edge clipped by as much, and the tint), as LatinIME's
  KeyboardView overlays pressed keys on its cached keyboard buffer.
- Preview labels are made when the layout is built, draw their text
  directly instead of through St.Label's offscreen cache, stay above their
  own key and are only shown by opacity.
- The warm-up runs on a timer after the keyboard is shown (and for a layout
  built while open), repaints the whole stage for that frame, and draws the
  letter layers, every preview and the pressed cap once.
- The vocabulary is built in idle slices of at most 3 ms (two-letter index
  buckets, a frequency list decoded in slices), so a touch made while it
  loads never waits behind it.

Headless at 360x808 scale 3: 0 journal flushes in every press frame (was
13), press frames 2.8 ms median, 4.4 ms worst.

